 ### 3.8 MDC-1200 PTT ID (CONFIG_MDC1200; multi-mode derivative)
 
 An FM channel can identify itself with an MDC-1200 data burst when a transmission starts, when PTT is released, or
-both. The channel holds the settings (`open-firmware-cps.md` 7.3.3.2): the ID type (none, MDC-1200), when it is sent,
-and the 16-bit unit ID. There is no radio-wide ID: a channel with no ID sends nothing. Received bursts are not
-decoded yet; the channel's decode option is stored for it.
+both, and can decode the bursts it receives to show who is calling. The channel holds the settings
+(`open-firmware-cps.md` 7.3.3.2): the ID type (none, MDC-1200), when it is sent, the 16-bit unit ID, and whether
+received IDs are decoded. There is no radio-wide ID: a channel with no ID sends nothing. Sending and decoding are
+separate: a channel can do either, both or neither.
 
 The burst (`core/mdc/mdc1200.c`):
 
@@ -405,6 +406,39 @@ the switch needs `CONFIG_APRS`. The microphone is muted while a burst plays; the
 With APRS built, the burst's 26 bytes of tones use the APRS transmit buffer: PTT has ended any APRS transmission, and
 none starts during one of ours. Untested on air: the pretimes and the 12.5 kHz level are first values.
 
+#### Decoding (CONFIG_MDC1200 with CONFIG_APRS_RX)
+
+On a channel set to decode, the board's receive audio (`hal_afsk_t` rx: 8 kHz, not squelched, the path APRS receive
+uses) runs through `mdc1200_dec` every poll while the channel is received.
+
+| Step | How |
+| --- | --- |
+| Bits | The tones are phase continuous, so the wave ends each bit rising or falling as the bit is 1 or 0. The bit's 7 samples are correlated with a 1200 Hz and an 1800 Hz sine ending at the bit's end; the sign of the stronger is the bit. A 250 Hz high pass comes first (hum, sub-audio tones) |
+| Timing | No clock recovery: 6 bit timings a sixth of a bit apart run side by side, each with its own sync search. Holds for a baud rate within 0.1 % |
+| Sync | The last 40 bits against the sync word, 3 bits may differ; all but 3 differing is the word inverted (an inverting audio path), and the data is inverted back |
+| Data | 112 bits de-interleaved; each data bit is in four parity checks, and one with three or more failing is flipped (majority decoding); then the CRC must match. A failed CRC gives nothing |
+| Result | The unit ID of any packet (a PTT ID at either end of a transmission, or another message type); the other timings drop the same burst |
+
+| Rule | Value |
+| --- | --- |
+| Runs | FM channel with decode on, receiving (not transmitting, PTT up), no scan or dual watch, no hotspot, not AM or broadcast receive |
+| APRS | On the frequency APRS receive listens on, the audio path is APRS's and nothing is decoded |
+| Squelch | Decoded with the squelch closed too (another group's tone, a weak signal) |
+| Battery saver | Off while the decoder runs: a sleeping receiver would miss the burst at the start |
+| Speaker | The channel's audio plays through the modem's codec while the decoder runs, as on the APRS frequency (the board's `hal_afsk_t` rx route) |
+| Memory | The decoder's 180 bytes and the sample buffer are the idle APRS demodulator's (`aprs_rx_scratch`); the board's audio ring comes from the heap while it runs |
+
+What the screen shows uses the view's digital voice fields (`radio_view_t.dv`), as a P25 channel does: `source` is the
+ID heard in the reception in progress, `last_source` and `last_ms` the last one heard on the channel.
+
+| When | Home screen |
+| --- | --- |
+| Receiving, an ID heard in this reception (or up to 1 s before the squelch opened) | `ID 0644` under the meter |
+| Idle, an ID heard on this channel | `Last ID 0644 2s ago` |
+| Channel change | The last ID is forgotten |
+
+The ID is 4 hex digits. There are no names for IDs yet. Untested on a radio.
+
